Here is a simple, real-world example of how these three tables work together over a 24-hour cycle.
Let’s imagine it is the end of the day today, September 7, 2026.
The Setup: The Raw Data (in Druid)
Millions of transactions happened today. In the Druid master database, you have raw balances for Branch B001 (which belongs to Circle C01).
Druid has these raw rows for Branch B001:
 * Account 1: Head Code HC001 = $1,000
 * Account 2: Head Code HC001 = $500
 * Account 3: Head Code HC202 = $2,000
 * Account 4: Head Code HC999 = $300 (Wait, the business just created this code today!)
Step 1: Reading the Rulebook (FINANCIAL_CATEGORY_MAPPING)
At night, the PySpark batch job wakes up. It doesn't know what HC001 or HC202 means. It looks at your Mapping Table to find out.
| SOURCE_CODE | TYPE | DESCRIPTION | MULTIPLIER |
|---|---|---|---|
| HC001 | DEMAND DEPOSITS | Saving Bank A/c | 1 |
| HC202 | DEMAND DEPOSITS | Current A/c | 1 |
| UNMAPPED_DEFAULT | UNCLASSIFIED | Unclassified Deposits | 1 |
What PySpark does:
 * It sees HC001 is "Saving Bank A/c".
 * It sees HC202 is "Current A/c".
 * It cannot find HC999. So, it follows our "Strict Reconciliation" rule and assigns it to UNMAPPED_DEFAULT.
Step 2: Crushing the Data (FINANCIAL_DAILY_METRIC)
PySpark applies the multipliers, adds the numbers together, and inserts the clean results into the Daily Metric table.
This is what gets saved to Oracle for September 7:
| BUSINESS_DATE | DOMAIN | DESCRIPTION | CIRCLE_CODE | BRANCH_CODE | AMOUNT |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 2026-09-07 | DEPOSIT | Saving Bank A/c | C01 | B001 | $1,500 |
| 2026-09-07 | DEPOSIT | Current A/c | C01 | B001 | $2,000 |
| 2026-09-07 | DEPOSIT | Unclassified Deposits | C01 | B001 | $300 |
Notice how 4 raw rows became 3 clean business rows. Across a whole bank, hundreds of millions of raw rows shrink down to a few thousand clean rows per day.
Step 3: Looking into the Future (FINANCIAL_FORECAST)
Right after PySpark finishes, the Forecast Batch Job wakes up.
It looks at the FINANCIAL_DAILY_METRIC table and says: "Show me the last 4 months of 'Saving Bank A/c' for Branch B001."
It runs its ARIMA math and predicts that in 30 days (Oct 7, 2026), the balance will grow. It saves this to the Forecast table:
| FORECAST_DATE | DOMAIN | DESCRIPTION | SCOPE_LEVEL | SCOPE_ID | PREDICTED_AMOUNT |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 2026-10-07 | DEPOSIT | Saving Bank A/c | BRANCH | B001 | $1,650 |
Step 4: The User Logs In (The Dashboard API)
The next morning, the Branch Manager for B001 opens the React Dashboard.
React asks the Dashboard Service for the "Savings Account Trend & Forecast".
Because of this architecture, the Dashboard Service does absolutely zero heavy lifting:
 * It runs a fast, indexed query on FINANCIAL_DAILY_METRIC to get the historical trend (ending at the $1,500 actual balance).
 * It runs a fast, indexed query on FINANCIAL_FORECAST to get the future trend (the $1,650 prediction).
 * It hands both lists to React in milliseconds.
The Result: The dashboard loads instantly. The totals match Druid perfectly because we caught the missing HC999 in the Unclassified bucket. The frontend draws a solid line for the past, and a dotted line for the future.
